/* Kenya Grade 10 Physics 1.2 Pressure — additional depth pack. */
(()=> {
 const c=window.CBE_COUNTRY_NOTES?.Kenya?.levels?.["Grade 10"]?.Physics?.find(x=>x.id==="ke-g10-phy-12-pressure");
 if(!c||c.__pressureDepthPack20261116)return;
 c.__pressureDepthPack20261116=true;c.sections=c.sections||[];
 c.sections.push(
 {heading:"Pressure in liquids: depth, density and gravitational field strength",paragraphs:[
 "A liquid at rest exerts pressure in all directions. The pressure at a point is caused by the weight of liquid above that point and is influenced by the liquid's density, depth and the gravitational field strength. For a liquid of uniform density, the gauge pressure is p = ρgh, where ρ is density in kilograms per cubic metre, g is gravitational field strength in newtons per kilogram and h is vertical depth below the free surface in metres. The product has units kg m⁻¹ s⁻², which is equivalent to the pascal.",
 "The equation predicts that pressure increases linearly with depth when density and g remain constant. It does not depend on the shape or total width of the container. A narrow tube and a wide tank containing the same liquid to the same vertical height produce the same pressure at points at equal depths. The total force on a surface does depend on its area: for uniform pressure acting normally, force equals pressure multiplied by area.",
 "The equation gives gauge pressure, measured above atmospheric pressure. Absolute pressure is the sum of atmospheric pressure and gauge pressure. This distinction matters when using pressure gauges, designing sealed containers or interpreting a barometer. In calculations, identify whether the question asks for pressure due to the liquid alone or total absolute pressure."
 ]},
 {heading:"Hydrostatic paradox and the shape of containers",paragraphs:[
 "The pressure at a given depth in a stationary liquid depends on vertical depth, density and gravitational field strength, not on the total volume of liquid or the shape of the vessel. This can appear surprising because differently shaped containers may hold different amounts of liquid while exerting the same pressure at their bases when the liquid height is equal. The side walls also exert forces on the liquid; vertical components of these forces help balance the weight of the liquid.",
 "The hydrostatic result assumes the liquid is at rest, has approximately uniform density and is subject to a nearly constant gravitational field. It is not a statement that the total force on every container base is identical. If base areas differ, the force differs because force equals pressure multiplied by area. Nor does it mean the walls experience identical forces: the pressure varies with depth and the wall area and orientation matter.",
 "A useful classroom demonstration uses connected vessels of different shapes. When the same liquid is poured into them and allowed to settle, the free surfaces reach the same horizontal level. This occurs because a difference in pressure at equal depths would cause fluid to flow until equilibrium is restored. The demonstration helps distinguish pressure from the total weight of liquid."
 ]},
 {heading:"Atmospheric pressure, barometers and weather",paragraphs:[
 "The atmosphere has mass and is attracted by Earth's gravity. Its weight produces atmospheric pressure on surfaces. Atmospheric pressure decreases with increasing altitude because the amount of air above a location becomes smaller and air density also changes with height. At sea level, standard atmospheric pressure is approximately 101,325 Pa, although actual pressure varies with weather and elevation.",
 "A mercury barometer balances atmospheric pressure against the pressure due to a column of mercury. In the simple model, atmospheric pressure equals ρgh for the mercury column, with the top of the column close to a vacuum. A liquid with a lower density would require a taller column to balance the same pressure. Aneroid barometers use a sealed flexible chamber that changes shape as external pressure changes.",
 "Weather systems are associated with pressure differences in the atmosphere. Air tends to move from regions of higher pressure toward lower pressure, although Earth's rotation and other effects influence wind direction. A falling barometer reading may be associated with an approaching low-pressure system, but a single reading does not provide a complete forecast. Meteorologists combine pressure measurements with temperature, humidity, wind and satellite observations."
 ]},
 {heading:"Hydraulic machines and Pascal's principle",paragraphs:[
 "Pascal's principle states that a change in pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted throughout the fluid. A hydraulic system uses this principle to transmit force. If two pistons have areas A₁ and A₂ and friction and height differences are neglected, equal pressure changes give F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂. A larger output piston can therefore exert a larger force than the input piston.",
 "The force multiplication does not create energy. The larger output piston moves a shorter distance than the smaller input piston. In an ideal system, input work equals output work: F₁d₁ = F₂d₂. Real hydraulic machines have losses due to friction, fluid leakage and deformation, so useful output work is less than input work. The hydraulic jack, vehicle lift and hydraulic braking system are familiar applications.",
 "Hydraulic brakes use a master cylinder to create pressure in brake fluid when the driver presses the pedal. The pressure is transmitted through pipes to wheel cylinders or callipers, where pistons press brake pads against a rotating disc or shoes against a drum. The friction converts kinetic energy into thermal energy. Brake fluid must be suitable for the operating temperature and should not contain air bubbles, because compressible gas would make the pedal feel soft and reduce effective force transmission."
 ]},
 {heading:"Upthrust, flotation and Archimedes' principle",paragraphs:[
 "An object immersed in a fluid experiences an upward resultant force called upthrust or buoyant force. Pressure increases with depth, so the fluid pushes more strongly on the lower surface than on the upper surface. Archimedes' principle states that the upthrust equals the weight of fluid displaced by the immersed object. The principle applies to liquids and gases.",
 "An object floats when the upthrust can balance its weight. A floating object displaces a quantity of fluid whose weight equals the object's weight. An object may be made from a material denser than water and still float if its overall shape encloses enough air to make its average density less than that of the displaced water. This explains why a steel ship can float even though a solid block of steel sinks.",
 "The fraction of a floating object's volume that is submerged depends on the ratio of its average density to the fluid density for a uniform fluid and a stable floating condition. If the fluid becomes denser, less volume needs to be displaced to provide the same upthrust. This is why a person may float more easily in very salty water than in fresh water."
 ]},
 {heading:"Pressure in gases and the kinetic particle model",paragraphs:[
 "Gas particles move rapidly and randomly, colliding with the walls of their container. Each collision changes the momentum of a particle and exerts a force on the wall. The combined effect of many collisions produces gas pressure. In a fixed volume, increasing the temperature increases the average kinetic energy of particles; collisions become more energetic and pressure rises if the amount of gas and volume remain constant.",
 "Boyle's law states that for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to volume. Therefore pV is constant, or p₁V₁ = p₂V₂, when the conditions are satisfied. Compressing a gas into a smaller volume increases the frequency of wall collisions per unit area and raises pressure. The law is an idealised model and real gases may deviate at high pressures or low temperatures.",
 "A syringe with its outlet sealed provides a simple qualitative demonstration of the pressure-volume relationship. Pushing the plunger reduces the trapped gas volume and increases the pressure, making the plunger harder to push. The experiment should be carried out gently with a suitable syringe and without directing it toward anyone. Temperature should be kept approximately constant for a fair test."
 ]},
 {heading:"Pressure measurement, units and instrument selection",paragraphs:[
 "The pascal is the SI unit of pressure and is equal to one newton per square metre. Other units include the kilopascal and bar. One kilopascal is 1,000 pascals, while one bar is 100,000 pascals. A pressure value is meaningful only when the reference is clear: gauge pressure is measured relative to local atmospheric pressure, while absolute pressure is measured relative to a vacuum.",
 "A manometer compares pressure using the difference in height of liquid columns. A simple U-tube manometer can measure the pressure difference between a gas supply and the atmosphere. For a liquid of density ρ and a vertical column difference h, the pressure difference is ρgh when the relevant assumptions apply. The reading depends on the vertical height difference, not the length of the curved tube.",
 "Different instruments are selected for different ranges and conditions. Tyre pressure gauges measure pressure relative to the atmosphere; barometers measure atmospheric pressure; industrial transducers may convert pressure into an electrical signal. Calibration, range, resolution, temperature limits and the fluid or gas being measured all influence instrument choice. A gauge should never be used beyond its rated pressure."
 ]},
 {heading:"Pressure applications in engineering, health and everyday life",paragraphs:[
 "Dams are generally made thicker near their bases because water pressure increases with depth. Engineers must account for the pressure distribution, total water force, foundation conditions and the possibility of seepage. Divers experience increasing pressure as they descend, which affects gas spaces in the body and the equipment they use. Safe diving requires training, controlled ascent and appropriate breathing-gas procedures.",
 "In medicine, blood pressure is a measure of the pressure exerted by circulating blood on artery walls. It is usually reported as systolic pressure over diastolic pressure, reflecting different phases of the cardiac cycle. The measurement depends on the cuff size, body position, rest, device calibration and technique. A single reading should be interpreted in context rather than treated as a complete diagnosis.",
 "Everyday examples include drinking through a straw, suction cups, vacuum packaging and water supply systems. When a person drinks through a straw, pressure in the mouth is reduced and atmospheric pressure on the liquid surface pushes the drink up the straw. The straw does not pull liquid upward by itself. This explanation illustrates the role of pressure differences and helps correct the common misconception that suction is a separate upward force."
 ]}
 );
 c.questions=c.questions||[];
 c.questions.push(
 {q:"A tank contains water to a depth of 2.4 m. Calculate the gauge pressure at the bottom. Take density as 1,000 kg m⁻³ and g as 10 N kg⁻¹.",a:"p = ρgh = 1,000 × 10 × 2.4 = 24,000 Pa = 24 kPa.",marks:3,markingPoints:["Correct equation p = ρgh (1)","Correct substitution with SI units (1)","24,000 Pa or 24 kPa (1)"]},
 {q:"Explain why a dam wall is usually thicker at its base than at its top.",a:"Water pressure increases with depth. The lower part experiences greater pressure and a larger resultant force, so the wall needs greater strength and thickness near its base.",marks:3,markingPoints:["Pressure increases with depth (1)","Lower wall experiences greater force/loading (1)","Greater thickness provides required strength/stability (1)"]},
 {q:"A hydraulic lift has an input piston area of 0.005 m² and an output piston area of 0.20 m². If the input force is 150 N, calculate the ideal output force.",a:"F₂ = F₁A₂/A₁ = 150 × 0.20/0.005 = 6,000 N.",marks:3,markingPoints:["Uses equal pressure relation (1)","Correct area ratio/substitution (1)","6,000 N (1)"]},
 {q:"State two reasons why a real hydraulic machine is not 100% efficient.",a:"Friction in moving parts, fluid leakage, heating, and deformation of components cause energy losses.",marks:2,markingPoints:["Any two valid energy-loss mechanisms (1 each)"]},
 {q:"Distinguish gauge pressure from absolute pressure.",a:"Gauge pressure is measured relative to local atmospheric pressure. Absolute pressure is measured relative to a vacuum; absolute pressure equals gauge pressure plus atmospheric pressure.",marks:2,markingPoints:["Correct gauge reference (1)","Correct absolute reference/relation (1)"]},
 {q:"A floating block displaces 0.003 m³ of water. Calculate the upthrust using water density 1,000 kg m⁻³ and g = 10 N kg⁻¹.",a:"Upthrust = weight of displaced water = ρVg = 1,000 × 0.003 × 10 = 30 N.",marks:3,markingPoints:["Archimedes' principle or formula (1)","Correct substitution (1)","30 N upward (1)"]}
 );
})();
